WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.191
Picture yourself scrolling through your bank app, and stopping.

2
00:00:07.191 --> 00:00:12.323
The same 189.99, at the same electronics shop, twice, two minutes apart.

3
00:00:12.323 --> 00:00:13.963
You were charged twice.

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.796
Support promises a refund, and to find every other customer it happened to.

5
00:00:18.796 --> 00:00:24.005
For that, the database must keep every payment, and write something next to each one.

6
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:29.564
So how do you put a number worked out from many rows onto every row, and lose none of them?

7
00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:31.427
Keep that double charge in mind.

8
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.945
Let's start smaller.

9
00:00:32.945 --> 00:00:39.604
The company's app keeps each payment as one row in a table called payments, like one line in a spreadsheet.

10
00:00:39.604 --> 00:00:42.280
Here are the eight that succeeded in March.

11
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:49.664
Each row has the payment's number, the shop that was paid, and the amount in cents.

12
00:00:49.664 --> 00:00:51.553
So 18999 means 189.99.

13
00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:58.340
And there's our double charge: payments 17 and 18, at shop 4, Volt Electronics.

14
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:03.992
Today's request: show every March payment, each one next to its shop's March total.

15
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:08.679
An earlier lesson's tool for one number from many rows is GROUP BY.

16
00:01:08.679 --> 00:01:11.314
It folds each shop's rows into one line.

17
00:01:11.314 --> 00:01:13.289
Eight rows in, four lines out.

18
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:19.867
Ask for the payment number as well, and PostgreSQL, the database program here, refuses with an error.

19
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.197
And that's fair.

20
00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:24.255
After folding, a line stands for a whole shop.

21
00:01:24.255 --> 00:01:26.581
Shop 4's line holds three payments.

22
00:01:26.581 --> 00:01:28.309
Which one should it print?

23
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:33.135
You could work out the totals separately, then join them back onto the payments.

24
00:01:33.135 --> 00:01:37.988
It works, but it's folding the laundry, then unfolding it all again to find one sock.

25
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:42.080
What we want is to keep every row, and write the total beside it.

26
00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:45.224
Picture a teacher marking a pile of exam papers.

27
00:01:45.224 --> 00:01:53.260
For each paper, she looks through the papers from the same class, writes the class average on this paper, and puts it back on the pile.

28
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.700
Every paper comes back with one more number, and none goes missing.

29
00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.956
Ours writes a total, not an average.

30
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:01.120
Let's map it.

31
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.278
A row is a paper.

32
00:02:02.278 --> 00:02:03.778
The class is the shop.

33
00:02:03.778 --> 00:02:06.982
The papers she reads are the rows of that shop.

34
00:02:06.982 --> 00:02:09.845
And the number she writes is a new column.

35
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:11.874
GROUP BY is the head teacher.

36
00:02:11.874 --> 00:02:16.063
She wants one line per class, and never sees a single paper again.

37
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:18.740
In SQL, the teacher is one word.

38
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:25.876
Write OVER after sum, then say which rows belong together: PARTITION BY merchant_id, the same shop.

39
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.900
Before we run it, a guess.

40
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.047
sum folds many values into one.

41
00:02:30.047 --> 00:02:33.787
So do we get four rows, eight rows, or the same error?

42
00:02:33.787 --> 00:02:34.895
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:38.738
Eight.

44
00:02:38.738 --> 00:02:40.439
Let's watch why.

45
00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:45.490
First, PostgreSQL sorts the rows, so each shop's payments sit together.

46
00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.671
Nothing is folded.

47
00:02:46.967 --> 00:02:49.432
Then it walks down the rows.

48
00:02:49.432 --> 00:02:55.684
Payment 14 is the only one at shop 1, so it reads this row alone: 5310.

49
00:02:55.933 --> 00:02:59.467
Payment 17 is at shop 4.

50
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:02.854
It reads 17, 18 and 19.

51
00:03:02.854 --> 00:03:09.628
Three times 18999 is 56997, written on row 17.

52
00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:14.102
Payment 18 reads the same rows, so it gets the same total.

53
00:03:14.102 --> 00:03:16.000
And row 17 is still there.

54
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.359
A column was added, and no row was taken away.

55
00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:22.233
Row 19 gets the same.

56
00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:30.527
Then 13 starts shop 5, so the set changes to 13, 16 and 22: 147500.

57
00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:32.747
The rest fill in the same way.

58
00:03:32.747 --> 00:03:34.727
Eight rows in, eight rows out.

59
00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:35.990
Now the names.

60
00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.947
The class is called a partition.

61
00:03:37.947 --> 00:03:40.944
The rows read for the current row are its window.

62
00:03:40.944 --> 00:03:45.592
And a function that reads a window, and keeps the row, is a window function.

63
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.756
For every row, a window of related rows.

64
00:03:48.756 --> 00:03:50.972
The function reads the window.

65
00:03:50.972 --> 00:03:52.007
The row stays.

66
00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:56.111
Now the total sits on the row, you can divide by it.

67
00:03:56.111 --> 00:04:00.359
Each of Volt's three payments is 33.3 percent of its March.

68
00:04:00.733 --> 00:04:02.757
Here the teacher picture breaks.

69
00:04:02.757 --> 00:04:04.971
She looks things up paper by paper.

70
00:04:04.971 --> 00:04:11.612
PostgreSQL first sorts the whole pile into shop order, and on a big table, that sort is most of the work.

71
00:04:11.867 --> 00:04:13.934
So pick by what you want back.

72
00:04:13.934 --> 00:04:15.932
One line per group: GROUP BY.

73
00:04:15.932 --> 00:04:19.033
Every row, with a number beside it: a window.

74
00:04:19.333 --> 00:04:22.266
So, a number from many rows, on every row?

75
00:04:22.266 --> 00:04:23.872
Give each row a window.

76
00:04:23.872 --> 00:04:25.548
GROUP BY folds the rows.

77
00:04:25.548 --> 00:04:29.109
A window adds a column, and never takes a row away.

78
00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:30.948
And the double charge?

79
00:04:30.948 --> 00:04:37.130
Partition by card, shop and amount, in time order, and payment 18 sits right after 17.

80
00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.227
Later in this lesson, a window function compares the two.

81
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:42.709
One quick check.

82
00:04:42.709 --> 00:04:47.393
Leave the brackets after OVER empty: no partition rule at all.

83
00:04:47.393 --> 00:04:48.979
What lands on row 21?

84
00:04:48.979 --> 00:04:50.188
Pause and think.

85
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.322
The whole month's total, 211132.

86
00:04:56.322 --> 00:05:02.715
With no rule, every row is related, so the whole result is one window.
